rdt.section: Architecture
title: $:/plugins/rimir/kind/doc/architecture
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<$transclude $tiddler="$:/plugins/rimir/doc-template/templates/page">
<$fill $name="body">

!! Schema model

|!Element |!Stored as |!Discovery |
|Type |One tiddler with fields `kind.type`, `kind.caption`, `kind.icon`, `kind.namespaces`, `kind.title-formula`, `kind.stream-fields`, `kind.fields` (JSON) |`[has[kind.type]has[kind.fields]]` |
|Field def |Entry inside the type's `kind.fields` JSON array |`[<type-tid>get[kind.fields]jsonindexes[]]` |
|Instance |Any tiddler with `kind.type: <type-key>` |`[kind.type<type-key>]` |

There are no separate per-field tiddlers, no inheritance slots, no enum tiddlers. The type tiddler is the single source of truth for its schema.

!! Namespace mechanism

* Each type's `kind.namespaces` field is a space-separated list of namespace tokens — a type can belong to many at once.
* The active namespace lives in `$:/config/rimir/kind/active-namespace` (single value; empty = all).
* All UI surfaces that enumerate types — //creator//, //overview//, //validator// — filter via the `kind.belongs-to-ns` function in `engine.tid`.

!! Field types (`typed` values)

|!`typed` |!Editor |!Storage |
|`text` |single-line `<$edit-text>` |plain string |
|`longtext` |textarea |plain string |
|`number` |numeric input |plain string |
|`date` |`<$edit-date>` |TW date format |
|`datetime` |`<$edit-date>` with time |TW datetime format |
|`url` |single-line `<$edit-text type="url">` |plain string; live format check requires `http://` or `https://` prefix |
|`email` |single-line `<$edit-text type="email">` |plain string; live format check `local@domain.tld` |
|`phone` |single-line `<$edit-text type="tel">` |plain string; live format check for German numbers (`+49` or `0` prefix) |
|`enum` |single: `<$select>`; multi: pill toggle row |single string or list-field |
|`ref` |typeahead (filter input + dropdown + selected pills with ×) |single tiddler title or list-field |
|`$title` |read-only |computed from `kind.title-formula` |
|`$calculated` |read-only |computed from the field's `formula` |

Extension point: `field-input.tid` is one `<%if [<typed>match[X]]%>` cascade. Adding a field type = one new branch.

!! Constraints

|!Constraint |!Meaning |
|`required` |Must have a value (blocking) |
|`min:N` |Min N characters (text/longtext/url/email/phone) or N entries (multi-enum / multi-ref) |
|`max:N` |Max N characters or entries |
|`unique` |Unique among instances of the same type |
|`unique:wiki` |Unique wiki-wide (across all tiddlers) |
|''(intrinsic)'' |`url` / `email` / `phone` fields carry a built-in format check that fires when the value is non-blank. See `kind.viol-format-url` / `-email` / `-phone` in `engine.tid`. Regex patterns live in `_patterns.tid`. |

The validator reports any violation as a row in its bulk report. The editor shows live error messages below each input.

Save gating: the in-tiddler creator's Create button is disabled when `kind.any-invalid` returns non-empty (the disabled expression appends `then[yes]` because TW's `$button` widget only treats the literal string `"yes"` as truthy). The cascade-palette flows (`+ New <kind>` and spawn `+ Create new <ref-kind>`) emit a Warning row in place of the Save leaf when `any-invalid` fires — same dispatch pattern as the title-empty Warning row. Warning rows carry `"ca-after-fire":"keep"` so Enter is a no-op (the palette stays open and the user can keep editing).

!! Title generation

When `kind.title-formula` is set, the creator computes a new instance's title by evaluating the formula against the draft state tiddler:

```
\function kind.eval-title(type-tid, target-tid)
  [<type-tid>get[kind.title-formula]]
  :map[<target-tid>subfilter<currentTiddler>]
```

Example formula: `[get[parent]][[/]][get[abbrev]]+[join[]]` produces `parent-title/abbrev` style titles.

On save, `kind.finalize-create` uses `kind.create-title`: the formula result, else the kind's caption, else its key. Without that fallback an empty base title made `$action-createtiddler` reuse the draft's own `$:/state/…` title.

!! Inverse ref fields

A `ref` field def may carry `"inverse": "<field>"`. `inverse.js` (library) keeps the other end in sync: it snapshots the ref values of every instance whose kind has such fields, listens to wiki `change` events, diffs old vs. new values and adds/removes the source title in the target's `<field>`. `inverse-startup.js` attaches it in the browser only — the server gets the mirrored writes through the normal sync.

* Drafts are skipped — both TW edit drafts (`draft.of`) and kind's create-form drafts under `$:/state/` / `$:/temp/` (`schema.isInstance`, and `kind.instances-of` in wikitext). The mirror happens when the instance is saved, so a link pre-set in a create form never lands on the target while the form is open.
* Deleting or retyping an instance releases all its targets.
* A changed type tiddler only re-snapshots (no back-fill of existing data).
* A write that changes nothing is skipped, so a two-way pair stops after one round.

These are the only JS modules in kind: every other part is wikitext, but ref fields are written from many places (field editors, generated palette action strings, wizard drafts, other plugins), and only a change listener sees all of them.

!! Groups

A group kind names its members field in `kind.group-of`. Nothing is stored about groups on the linking side: a field that does not take the group kind but takes its member kind gets the members, once, when the group is linked. Later changes to the group do not propagate. The rule exists twice, kept in step: wikitext (`kind.group-for` / `kind.group-members-for`, used by the ref picker, the palette's multi-ref stage and spawns) and JS (`schema.groupMembers`, used by rimir/ports). Single fields never take groups, groups are one level (a member that is a group is dropped), and a field whose `ref-types` is empty (any kind) links groups as themselves.

!! Spawns

`kind.spawns` reuses the cockpit's create flow: `kind.gm-create-linked` with `dir=in` sets the link field, and `copy=yes` copies the host's values for every field of the new kind except the link field, computed fields and fields with a `default` (the kind's own default wins). Ref values go through `kind.ref-link-one`: kept if accepted, expanded if a group, else dropped. `kind.apply-defaults-to` runs afterwards, so defaults can build on the link and the copied values (e.g. a caption from the parent's caption).

!! New kind instance

`create-button.tid` is a PageControls button with a popup. The kind list (`kind.create-menu-types`: all kinds of the active namespace, sorted by `kind.type-label`) is inside the `$reveal`, so it renders only while the popup is open. A pick calls `kind.gm-create` without a host and with `after-create="kind.gm-link-created"`, which opens the saved instance (no link to write without a host). `kind.gm-add-child` is `kind.gm-create` with a host, so the graph tooltip, the dashboard and the toolbar share one create path. The create form passes `focus-first="yes"` to the editor, which sets `autofocus` on the row of `kind.focus-key` (the first field whose input takes `focus`: not computed, not date/datetime/enum). A `$keyboard` around the whole modal closes it on ''Esc'' in every mode (same as Close / Cancel); it fires while the cursor is inside the modal. The popup starts with a search field (`$:/temp/rimir/kind/create-search`, cleared on every open and pick) that has `focus="yes"`: the `$button` re-renders when the popup opens, so without a focused input inside the popup no keyboard widget would ever see Esc. `create.matches` (local function) runs `kind.create-menu-match`; the input sits outside every `$let` that depends on the search text, because a `$let` re-renders its children when a value changes and that would rebuild the input on each keystroke. Enter works out the first match when it fires. Picking (click or Enter) goes through the local `kind.create-pick`. The icon is a stroke SVG; page controls colour icons through `fill`, so styles.tid sets `fill: none` and the stroke colour for `.tc-image-create-kind`.

!! Focus

`focus-button.tid` is a ViewToolbar button. Rendering it costs only the type lookup that decides whether it shows (`kind.cockpit-host` + `kind.focus-on`); the rules run in the click action (`kind.focus-actions`), which writes `$:/StoryList` directly, like the core close-others. The rules tiddler (`$:/config/rimir/kind/focus/<kind-key>`, else the shipped default) is split into lines; `kind.focus-children` evaluates each line through `:map:flat[all[tiddlers]subfilter<line>!sort[modified]]`, so every rule is an independent filter (a `:filter` run stays inside its line) that starts from all tiddlers like a top-level filter (inside `:map` a bare first step would otherwise see only the rule string), sorted on its own. `+[unique[]]` keeps the first occurrence, which gives the per-rule grouping. Streams nodes also carry `parent`; the host's own rows are in its `stream-list` and are excluded, so only separately created children (tasks, notes, meetings, …) open.

Shift-click: `$button` sets `<<modifier>>` only for its `actions` attribute, not for action widgets among its children, so the button calls `kind.focus-actions` from `actions` and passes `add=yes` when the modifier contains `shift`. In that mode two `action-listops` run: the first removes the found tiddlers from the story, the second rebuilds it as `allbefore<host>`, host, found tiddlers, `allafter<host>`. They run one after the other, so the second sees the first's write. The two modes are two positive `<%if%>`s, not `<%if%>`/`<%else%>`: actions in an `<%else%>` branch never fire.

!! Parent button

`parent-button.tid` is a ViewToolbar button next to focus. Rendering it costs one lookup, `kind.up-target`: the instance must be a kind instance (`kind.cockpit-host`, so Streams nodes — which also carry `parent` — get no button), and `parent` must name an existing tiddler other than the instance (a title-list value uses its first entry). The click (`kind.up-actions`) works like Shift-focus: one `action-listops` takes the parent out of the story, a second rebuilds it as `allbefore<host>`, parent, host, `allafter<host>`; then it navigates to the parent. An instance that is not in the story only navigates. `parent` is hard-coded, like the rest of kind (add child, auto-children, focus default rules, spawns).

!! Instance filter

`kind.visible(surface)` is the one place where the wiki's visibility rule meets kind's lists: `[subfilter<kind.instance-filter-text>]`, where `kind.instance-filter-text` reads `$:/config/rimir/kind/instance-filter` and checks `<surface>` against `…/apply-to`; otherwise it returns the pass-all filter `[!is[blank]]` (stored in `_brackets!!pass`, since a literal with brackets cannot be written in a function body). Without a config tiddler that is one lookup per list. The function receives the list as its input, so call sites append `+[function[kind.visible],[pickers]]`. Applied to `kind.ref-candidates` and `kind.ref-group-candidates` (every picker built on them: field-input, palette, wizard flat list, rimir/ports), the wizard tree picker (`kind.wiz-root-nodes`, `kind.wiz-child-nodes`), the cockpit's incoming rows (`kind.cockpit-incoming`) and the overview's node filter. Linked values are not filtered: the field bar and the editor pills read the field, the cockpit's outgoing sections use `kind.field-display-list`, and the palette's toggle/leaf lists use `kind.ref-candidates-keep` (candidates ∪ already linked, via a per-row `:filter`).

The filter runs as a `subfilter` in the caller's widget scope, so `$:/tags/Global` functions resolve where the page template imported them (`$:/core/config/GlobalImportFilter`). The cascade palette mounts its own widget tree and imports only `$:/tags/Macro`; there a Global function is not found (TODO in engine.tid; to be fixed in cascade-palette).

!! Ref dropdown keyboard

`field-input.tid` wraps the ref search input in a `$keyboard` (down up enter escape) whose actions call `kind.ref-key` with `<<event-key>>`. Mouse and keys share one entry list (`kind.ref-menu`) and one pick (`kind.ref-pick`); the highlight index is `<search-state>/hi`, reset by the input's `inputActions`. The conditions in the action procedures are positive `<%if%>` with `:filter` gates: an `:and` run that starts with a constructor (`[<q>…]`) ignores the accumulator and would make every branch fire. Only the pills read the field value (their own `$let cur`); the input's condition is `[<is-multi>!is[blank]] ~[…!has<field-key>…]`, constant for a multi field, so a pick does not re-render the input and the cursor stays for the next one. Esc with text clears the search (the `$keyboard` stops the event, so the graph modal stays open); Esc on an empty search runs `<<kind.esc-outer>>`, which the graph modal defines as its own close.

!! Cockpit

`cockpit.tid` (`$:/tags/ViewTemplate`, after `viewtemplate`) dispatches on the type's `kind.cockpit`: blank = generic panel, `none` = nothing, else a custom cockpit tiddler. The generic panel needs no per-model knowledge: outgoing sections come from the kind's own ref fields, incoming sections from every (source kind, field) whose `ref-types` name this kind. A source field whose `inverse` is a ref field of this kind is skipped there, because the same relation already shows as the outgoing field.

The generic panel sits behind a per-instance toggle (`$:/state/rimir/kind/cockpit/open/<instance>`). The toggle is rendered by stream-view at the end of the tiddler's field bar (only with `tiddler-bar=yes`, which viewtemplate sets); cockpit.tid renders the panel only while open. Both use the same gates (`kind.cockpit-host`, `kind.cockpit-generic-on`, `kind.cockpit-open`), so toggle and panel cannot disagree. While closed nothing of the panel is rendered; the sections (and their `kind.referencing-instances` / `kind.field-display-list` scans) are inside an `<%if%>` on the open state, so a collapsed panel costs only the type-level check (`kind.out-fields` / `kind.in-sources`) that decides whether the toggle appears. Custom cockpits are not wrapped; they control their own rendering.

The ＋ of a section reuses the graph-modal create form and the shared `kind.finalize-create` save tail (`kind.gm-create-linked`). The link to the host is written in one of two ways: incoming — pre-set in the draft, so the form shows it and required constraints pass; outgoing — written onto the host by the `after-create` hook (`kind.gm-link-created`), which has the saved title as `<<createTiddler-title>>`. The opposite end of an `inverse` pair is left to inverse sync (outgoing also pre-sets it in the draft so the form shows it).

Bridges can extend the flow without new modal code: optional `seed` (JSON of field values) is applied to the draft after link and copy but before `kind.apply-defaults-to`, and optional `after-link` (a global procedure name, with `ctx-a`/`ctx-b`) is stored on the modal state and run by `kind.gm-link-created` after the link step, with `<<createTiddler-title>>` in scope. Both default to no effect.

!! Stream-view (ViewTemplate auto-registration)

Each field icon in the bar names its instance and field: `data-kind-src`, `data-kind-field`, and `data-kind-ref="yes"` on ref fields (`kind.is-ref-typed`). Kind does nothing with them; they let bridges attach to a field without knowing kind's markup (rimir/ports drags new links out of ref field icons).

`viewtemplate.tid` is tagged `$:/tags/ViewTemplate` with `list-after: $:/core/ui/ViewTemplate/title`. On any tiddler with a `kind.type` field, it renders `stream-view.tid`, which reads `kind.stream-fields` from the type and emits a compact bar. Empty/missing `kind.stream-fields` = bar renders nothing (self-disabling).

A click on a field cell opens a popup containing the field's editor; a top-bar toggle switches to inline-edit mode (the editor replaces the cell directly).

!! Shared infrastructure

* ''engine.tid'' — all shared functions: type discovery, namespace filtering, JSON field-array accessors, constraint extraction, validation predicates, display helpers (truncation, ref resolution), title-formula evaluation
* ''field-input.tid'' — typed-dispatch input widget used by editor, stream-view popup, stream-view inline, and type-builder live preview
* ''editor.tid'' — 2-column table editor; takes only `target-tid` in scope; transcludes field-input per row

</$fill>
</$transclude>
